                <pb facs="0656" n="562" sameAs="frus:frus1930v03/pg562"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.543 Palmolive Company/11</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-01-11">January 11, 1930</date>.<lb/>[Received January
                        20.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2132</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to my despatch
                    No. 1994 of November 12, 1929, relative to the case of the Palmolive Company in
                    the Mexican courts, and the Company’s complaint that consideration had not been
                    given by the courts to the Convention for the Protection of Industrial Property,
                    revised at Washington June 2, 1911, to which both the United States and Mexico
                    are signatories. For the Department’s further information in regard to this
                    matter, I have the honor to forward herewith enclosed a copy of a letter, dated
                    January 10, 1930, which I have received from Basham &amp; Ringe, attorneys in
                    Mexico City for the Palmolive Company, together with a translation of the
                    decision of the Supreme Court in this case, the translation being furnished by
                    Basham &amp; Ringe.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herschel V.
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>



